C — Cross-VM Atomic Flow (coordinator) — docs/tutorials/cross-vm-atomic.md
Goal: Atomically transfer asset A (EVM token) to SVM and mint a wrapped token on SVM, in an atomic, rollback-safe way.

High level sequence:

User submits a cross-VM intent to the Router contract with targetVM: SVM and payload referencing the SVM program and accounts.

Router contract locks/escrows EVM tokens in a guarded contract (onchain lock with timeout).

Router emits an on-chain event the SVM operators ingest (or an oracle signs a proof).

SVM program verifies the proof, mints wrapped tokens to the destination account.

Router finalizes the escrow (burns/marks settled) after receiving proof; if timeout occurs, user can reclaim.

Simple safety pseudocode (router):

mapping(bytes32 => Escrow) escrows;

function initiateCrossVM(address token, uint256 amount, bytes32 target) external {
  transferFrom(msg.sender, address(this), amount);
  bytes32 id = keccak256(abi.encodePacked(msg.sender, block.number, ...));
  escrows[id] = Escrow(token, amount, msg.sender, false);
  emit EscrowInitiated(id, token, amount, msg.sender, target);
}
function finalizeEscrow(bytes32 id, bytes proof) external onlyRelayer {
  // verify proof that SVM minted wrapped token
  escrows[id].finalized = true;
}
function reclaim(bytes32 id) external {
  require(block.timestamp > escrows[id].timeout && !escrows[id].finalized);
  transfer(escrows[id].owner, escrows[id].amount);
}
SVM side: program must accept a signed proof (blockhash + event + relayer signature) and mint.

Atomicity notes:

Use optimistic relayer + timeout pattern (safe and common).

For true atomic on-chain across different VM semantics you need a two-phase commit across validators — expensive. Use off-chain signed relayer proofs to keep UX fast.

